<Project>

  <Import Project="$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove('Directory.Build.props', '$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../'))" />

  <!--
    The GC configuration the throughput work landed on (benchmarks/Incremental.Torture/README.md).
    A circuit keeps a large, mostly-static live set and allocates per step, so the GC's share of
    process CPU is the thing to tune; on the torture rig this configuration is worth more than any
    single code change (16.6 -> 11.6 us of CPU per record). Every example gets it by default so
    that what you measure running one is what the library actually does.

    Server GC, because operator state is sharded per worker and collected concurrently with the
    step. DATAS off (GarbageCollectionAdaptationMode 0), because it keeps trimming a heap whose
    live set is large and static, which turns into near-continuous gen2 work.

    The cost is working set: more heaps, none of them trimmed. That is the intended trade here.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ServerGarbageCollection>true</ServerGarbageCollection>
    <ConcurrentGarbageCollection>true</ConcurrentGarbageCollection>
    <GarbageCollectionAdaptationMode>0</GarbageCollectionAdaptationMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The third setting is the heap count, and it wants CircuitOptions.Workers rather than the core
    count: server GC otherwise gives a 4-worker circuit on a 20-thread machine 20 heaps and 20
    collection threads, most of them for memory no worker ever touches. It is startup
    configuration, so each example declares the worker count it builds its circuit with and it is
    baked into the runtimeconfig here. An example that takes the worker count from the command
    line has to set DOTNET_GCHeapCount instead (see Incremental.Showcase).
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(CircuitWorkers)' != ''">
    <RuntimeHostConfigurationOption Include="System.GC.HeapCount" Value="$(CircuitWorkers)" Trim="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
